<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter II of Cyprus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1357–<a href="/years/1382">1382</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lusignan">House of Lusignan</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> from 1369, after the murder of his father Peter I. At his coronation as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in Famagusta in 1372, a quarrel over precedence between Genoese and Venetian merchants led to riots in which Genoese were killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> retaliated with an invasion in 1373, and Peter had to cede Famagusta to Genoa and pay a heavy indemnity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-cyprus.jpg" alt="Peter II of Cyprus" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reinhard Saczewski · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zypern-_Peter_II._-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett,_Berlin_-_5538438.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peter, called the Fat, was the son of Peter I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and Eleanor of Aragon, and the eleventh king of Cyprus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lusignan">House of Lusignan</a>. He was declared king on 17 January 1369, but as he was still a minor his uncle John of Lusignan, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, ruled as regent. Eleanor believed John had been involved in her husband's murder and secretly asked European powers for military help to punish the killers. The Genoese answered, seeing a chance to gain power in Cyprus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Peter was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in the Cathedral of St Sophia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> on 6 January 1372 and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the Cathedral of St Nicholas in Famagusta on 10 October. The quarrel over which community should hold the right rein of the royal horse spread from the feast into the streets, and the Genoese were blamed and arrested. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> sent an expedition under Peter di Campofregoso, brother of the doge. To gather his forces, Peter gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a>, taken by his father, to Emir Teke. He left the resistance to his uncles John and James; Famagusta let the Genoese in, supposedly for negotiations, and the king and his mother were taken prisoner.</p><p>The Genoese attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limassol">Limassol</a> and Paphos and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, while John and James held out at Saint Hilarion Castle and Kyrenia. The settlement Peter was forced to accept also required James to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and the Genoese arrested him on his way. Before leaving they executed those accused of the murder of Peter I, and in 1375 Eleanor had Prince John killed. After her conflicts with Peter's wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentina-visconti">Valentina Visconti</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti">Bernabò Visconti</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, whom he had married by proxy in 1376, the king sent Eleanor back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1378.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Peter made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, improved the fortifications of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> and built a royal villa at Potamia. His reign saw the decline of the kingdom: the loss of its possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and of Famagusta, its chief harbour, which then declined. He died on 13 October 1382 at the Palace of La Cava in Nicosia and was buried at St Dominic's. His only daughter had died in infancy, and his uncle succeeded him as James I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter II of Cyprus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the coronation of King <b>Peter II of Cyprus</b> in Famagusta in 1372, a dispute over precedence between Genoese and Venetian merchants led to riots in which Genoese were killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/genoese-famagusta">Genoese Famagusta</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.17,33.37&amp;year=1373" aria-label="Nicosia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5395 892 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter II of Cyprus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5845 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5845 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.17,33.37&amp;year=1373">What was here in 1373? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
